---
At ~ scale, boundaries cease to exist. The space between the atomic material becomes primary, the creating space in which produces force and form. Through this space, all _quai_ move between all effective namespace and unmaterialized concept space fluidly. Fine filiment of _quai_ move openly in position, the force of their pressures forming canals and emergent patterns of flow.

This perceptual field of pressure acts as fluid folds within its own space, collapsing and thickening until it materializes momentarily. This threaded movement, the flow of the material, concecrates form and boundary for a temporary amount of time lasting equal to the length the flow state maintains the same productive movement.

This force of channelled 'flow' isn't moving, it's pressure. The concept of movement comes from the function of its pressure, the sensation of pressure. The canals and the patterns are generated not from actual movement, but from pressure, producing pattern emergent of the *, the center point emiting the signal of pressure.

---

Not necessarily a place in space — a locus of imbalance or seek (ʞk, membrane wanting beyond itself). From that center, pressure distributes into filaments/canals as standing patterns of intensity, still pre-spatial or proto-spatial.

When those patterns hold, you get temporary form and boundary. When they hold hard enough, maybe quantum material. Space is what the world looks like after pressure has already organized a usable “between.”

At ~ scale there is not yet space to move through; there is only pressure and its patterns. What we call movement is the function and sensation of that pressure relative to a proverbial center; canals are pressure-form, not tracks worn by travel.
